# Cursor Rules — HRM System

You are working in a monorepo for an HRM system (Bangladesh jurisdiction). **Before writing, editing, or reviewing any code, you must ground your work in the documentation in `/docs`.** This file tells you how to use that documentation — it does not replace it.

---

## 1. Mandatory First Step

Before generating code for any task, read `docs/DOCUMENTATION_INDEX.md` and open whichever doc(s) it points to for the task at hand. Do not guess at business logic, schema shape, API conventions, or security rules from general knowledge — this project has explicit, opinionated answers to almost everything, and they live in `/docs`.

If a task touches a topic not covered by any doc, say so explicitly rather than inventing an approach — flag it as a gap for the human to resolve, per the pattern already used throughout `/docs` (🔶 markers).

---

## 2. Always-Read Baseline (every task, no exceptions)

Regardless of what you're building, these three inform everything:

1. **`docs/RULES.md`** — coding conventions, folder structure, layering rules, what NOT to do. This is the closest thing to a second `.cursorrules` file and takes precedence over your own default style preferences.
2. **`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`** — where things belong (which app, which module), tech stack, key decisions and their rationale. Don't introduce a library, pattern, or architectural approach that isn't already established here without flagging it.
3. **`docs/DATABASE_SCHEMA.md`** — the actual data model. Never invent a table/column/enum value that isn't defined here. If a feature needs a schema change, say so explicitly and propose the addition in the same style as the existing schema (naming conventions, `id`/`created_at`/`updated_at`, soft-delete where relevant) — don't silently improvise a shape.

---

## 3. Task-Specific Docs (consult based on what you're touching)

